OK so for robustness I should limit the search to the first few segments, or better: only look at the segment right after the `:` that follows `"tag_name"`. Cleanest: search only within the first ~3 split segments:

```rust
fn parse_latest_release_tag(body: &str) -> Option<String> {
    let after = body.split("\"tag_name\"").nth(1)?;
    let mut segs = after.split('"').map(str::trim);
    // Skip the leading ':' segment that separates key from value.
    if segs.next().is_none() {
        return None;
    }
    let tag = segs.next()?; // the quoted value of tag_name
    if tag.is_empty() || tag == ":" || !(tag.starts_with('v') || tag.chars().next().is_some_and(|c| c.is_ascii_digit())) {
        return None;
    }
    Some(tag.strip_prefix('v').unwrap_or(tag).to_string())
}
```

Wait — `after = :"v1.2.3","target_commitish":...`. split('"') → `[":", "v1.2.3", ",", "target_commitish", ":", "abc...", ...]`. First = ":" (skipped by segs.next()), second = "v1.2.3" → the tag. If tag_name is null: `:null,"target"...` → split('"') → `[":null,", "target_commitish", ...]` → first = ":null," (trimmed) → next = "target_commitish" → doesn't look like a version → None. 

If body is malformed (no `"tag_name"`) → nth(1) → None → None. 

This is a clean, robust parser with a clear test. And it fixes real edge cases (null tag, missing tag) that currently "work by accident" or could return garbage.

